<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oswald of Northumbria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 604–<a href="/years/642">642</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Saint Oswald</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oswald was king of Northumbria from 634 to 642. A son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelfrith-of-northumbria">Æthelfrith of Bernicia</a>, he spent his youth in exile among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaels">Gaels</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a> and became a Christian on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a>. He won the throne by defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadwallon-ap-cadfan">Cadwallon of Gwynedd</a> at Heavenfield, brought the monk Aidan from Iona to found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a>, and was killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penda-of-mercia">Penda of Mercia</a> at Maserfield in 642. He was venerated as a saint.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oswald-of-northumbria.jpg" alt="Oswald of Northumbria" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robin Widdison at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Oswald_Durham_Cathedral.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Picts and to the English: in 635 the Iona monk Aidan founded Lindisfarne at the invitation of King <b>Oswald of Northumbria</b>, who had spent his exile among the Gaels, and bishops trained in the Iona tradition led the Northumbrian church until the Synod of Whitby in 664.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dal-riata">Dal Riata · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Cadwallon ap Cadfan, in alliance with Penda of Mercia, defeated and killed Edwin of Northumbria at the Battle of Hatfield Chase in 633 and ravaged Northumbria before he was himself killed by <b>Oswald</b> at the Battle of Heavenfield in 634.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-gwynedd">Kingdom of Gwynedd · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In alliance with the British king Cadwallon of Gwynedd he defeated and killed Edwin at the Battle of Hatfield Chase in 633, and in 642 he killed <b>Oswald of Northumbria</b> at Maserfield.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Kingdom of Mercia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Oswald</b> reunited them after his victory at Heavenfield in 634.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Christianity arrived when Bishop Birinus baptised King Cynegils in 635, with <b>Oswald of Northumbria</b> as godfather, and established a see at Dorchester-on-Thames; the see later moved to Winchester, and a second bishopric was created at Sherborne in 705 under the scholar Aldhelm.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Penda of Mercia, allied with the Welsh kingdom of Gwynedd, defeated King <b>Oswald of Northumbria</b>, probably at Oswestry in Shropshire.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-5">5 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
